  "narrative": "The metabolomics profiling revealed significant perturbations centred on **Glycine, serine and threonine metabolism (KEGG map00260)**, which emerged as the top candidate pathway with an FDR of 0.0 and a remarkable fold-enrichment of 603.3. Seven of the eleven input metabolites map to this pathway: choline (C00114), phosphoserine (C01005), adenosine monophosphate (C00020), S-adenosylmethionine (C00019), (R)-lipoic acid (C16241), L-cystathionine (C02291), and sarcosine (C00213). The enrichment is driven primarily by these hub metabolites participating in intersecting one-carbon and amino-acid interconversions.\n\n**Key driver compounds and their network positions**\n\nCholine acts as an upstream entry point; it feeds into the pathway through sequential oxidation to betaine and subsequent donation of methyl groups, eventually contributing the methyl donor S-adenosylmethionine (SAM). The KEGG graph confirms a four-reaction path from choline (C00114) to SAM (C00019) via betaine aldehyde and dimethylglycine intermediates. Phosphoserine (C01005) sits at the serine-biosynthesis branch and connects to sarcosine (C00213) via a three-hop route through L-serine and glycine, demonstrating tight coupling between one-carbon metabolism and trans-sulfuration. SAM is a central methyl-donor that couples to both phospholipid biosynthesis (via phosphorylcholine and choline) and the methionine cycle, linking the observed choline and phosphorylcholine perturbations to methylation capacity. L-Cystathionine (C02291) anchors the trans-sulfuration stream, and (R)-lipoic acid (C16241) serves as an essential cofactor for the glycine cleavage system, upstream of both serine and one-carbon folate cycles.\n\n**Biological significance**\n\nDisruption of this metabolic hub implies widespread consequences: impaired methyl-group cycling affects epigenetic regulation and phospholipid synthesis, while trans-sulfuration perturbation impacts redox homeostasis and detoxification capacity. The co-enrichment of choline, phosphorylcholine, and SAM points to altered phosphatidylcholine biosynthesis—consistent with membrane-remodelling or proliferative states. Combined with uridine 5′-monophosphate elevation (pyrimidine synthesis), the signature suggests a treatment-induced shift toward increased one-carbon demand, enhanced phospholipid turnover, and altered nucleotide metabolism, a pattern observed in contexts ranging from hepatic stress to proliferative cell states.",
